[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third question, on how the shared module gets loaded — this repo has two incompatible module worlds and no build step,
  so it's a real fork.

  Today [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m via a plain [38;5;153m<script src>[39m (browser globals, no [38;5;153mimport[39m), while [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m.
  A shared module has to be reachable from both the page and a Node test runner.

  - [1mDual export, no build[0m — one [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m that attaches to [38;5;153mwindow[39m for the page and sets [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m when [38;5;153mmodule[39m
    exists, so Node tests [38;5;153mrequire()[39m it. Zero deps, zero build, works with the existing [38;5;153m<script src>[39m tag. Slightly dated
    idiom, and the dual-export shim is a few lines of boilerplate.
  - [1mES modules throughout[0m — convert [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m to [38;5;153mimport[39m, test with Node's native ESM.
    Modern and clean, but it converts [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, and [38;5;153msrc/[39m stays CommonJS, so the repo ends up mixed anyway. Also means the
    page no longer works from [38;5;153mfile://[39m in some browsers.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — real answer for a growing app, wrong answer for four files and zero dependencies.

  I'd recommend dual export: it's the only one that changes nothing about how the page loads while still being testable
  in Node today, and it's cheap to migrate to ESM later if you add a build step.
